## Graphs

It should be easy to see that members of a boundary family tend to have the same graph. In fact, it should be easy to see when a mino has no 2x2s, the graphs are almost always the same, or split into a few sets of cases:

(TODO example with the wing hexomino)

Can we formalize this in any way, or determine what possible graphs there are for a given boundary family? Is there a further generalization we can make so that graphs with 2x2s belong in the same family as those without one?

## Tiling

Using directedness classes, we can rule out large swathes of polyominoes from tiling rectangles and other regions.

**Theorem** proper staircases, forks, diamonds, crescents, and range charts can never tile a rectangle.

The proof will be mostly left as an exercise to the reader, but the general argument is by infinite descent: fit a polyomino in the corner and show that the remaining space creates another corner that must be filled in the same way.

## Generalizations to other polyforms

What are the directedness classes for polyhexes, polyiamonds, and polykings? What are the corresponding boundary family classes?

## Generalizations to continuous sets

The polyomino definition of convexity is defined analogously to a [convex set](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convex_set). We can use analogous, continuous definitions of directedness to define generalizations to convexity for continuous sets.
